    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "order": 9,
      "bbox": [
        743,
        194,
        796,
        915
      ],
      "margin_number": 148,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "A common way of reporting what someone has said in Greek is by using a clause introduced by ὅτι, ‘that’, e.g.",
      "lang": "mixed",
      "para_no": 148,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "passage_line",
      "order": 10,
      "bbox": [
        808,
        246,
        826,
        910
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "λέγουσιν ὅτι ὁ Σωκράτης δεινός ἐστιν. ‘They say that Socrates is clever.’",
      "lang": "mixed",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "order": 11,
      "bbox": [
        837,
        194,
        891,
        915
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "Observe, however, that in the ὅτι-clause Greek preserves the original tense and mood of the utterance. You must therefore pay special attention when the verb introducing the ὅτι-clause is in the past. Note the following examples:",
      "lang": "mixed",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
